.TH DSTAT-GO 1 "2026-11-10" "dstat-go 1.0.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
dstat-go \- versatile resource statistics tool (Go implementation of dstat)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B dstat-go
[\-afv] [options..] [delay [count]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B dstat-go
is a from-scratch Go implementation of
.BR dstat (1)
0.8.0.  Options, column order, headers, units, colours, scaling and CSV output
follow the upstream tool; all data is read from the Linux
.I /proc
and
.I /sys
filesystems.
.PP
The program is a single statically linked binary with no Python interpreter or
plugin directory dependency.  It is installed as
.I /usr/bin/dstat-go
so that it can coexist with the
.B dstat
package provided by the distribution (on some distributions that file is a
Performance Co-Pilot wrapper script).
.PP
With no plugin selected the default set
.B \-cdngy
(cpu, disk, net, page, sys) is shown once per second.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-c, \-\-cpu
CPU usage: user, system, idle, wait, steal.
.TP
.B \-d, \-\-disk
Disk read/write throughput.
.TP
.B \-g, \-\-page
Paging activity (page in/out).
.TP
.B \-i, \-\-int
Hardware interrupts per second.
.TP
.B \-l, \-\-load
1, 5 and 15 minute load average.
.TP
.B \-m, \-\-mem
Memory usage: used, free, buffers, cache, available.
.TP
.B \-n, \-\-net
Network receive/send throughput.
.TP
.B \-p, \-\-proc
Process counts: runnable, uninterruptible, new.
.TP
.B \-r, \-\-io
Block I/O requests completed.
.TP
.B \-s, \-\-swap
Swap usage and free space.
.TP
.B \-t, \-\-time
Current date and time column.
.TP
.B \-T, \-\-epoch
Seconds since the epoch column.
.TP
.B \-y, \-\-sys
System statistics: interrupts, context switches.
.TP
.B \-a, \-\-all
Same as \-cdngy.
.TP
.B \-v, \-\-vmstat
vmstat-like set: proc, mem, page, disk, sys, cpu.
.TP
.B \-f, \-\-full
Show every device/interface instead of a total column.
.TP
.B \-\-bits
Show rates in bits per second instead of bytes.
.TP
.B \-\-float, \-\-integer
Force floating point or integer output.
.TP
.B \-\-noheaders
Do not repeat column headers on every update.
.TP
.B \-\-noupdate
Do not redraw the header at all after the first block.
.TP
.B \-\-bw, \-\-black-on-white
Colour theme for light-background terminals.
.TP
.B \-\-color, \-\-nocolor
Force colour output on or off.
.TP
.B \-o, \-\-output \fIFILE\fR
Append CSV output to
.IR FILE
while keeping the normal screen output.
.TP
.B \-\-pidfile \fIFILE\fR
Write the process id to
.IR FILE .
.TP
.B \-\-list
List the available internal plugins.
.TP
.B \-\-all-plugins
Enable every internal plugin.
.TP
.B \-h, \-\-help
Show help.
.TP
.B \-V, \-\-version
Show version, platform and terminal information.
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.I delay
Seconds between updates (default 1).
.TP
.I count
Number of updates to print; the default is unlimited.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B DSTAT_OPTS
Options prepended to the command line.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /proc/stat, /proc/meminfo, /proc/net/dev, /proc/diskstats, /proc/vmstat
Data sources read on every update.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/dstat-go/docs/dstat-spec.md
Behaviour specification (options, plugins, formulas, formats).
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/dstat-go/docs/verification-real.md
Real-hardware verification record against upstream dstat 0.8.0.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
.B dstat-go
Default plugins, one second interval.
.TP
.B dstat-go \-cdn 2 5
CPU, disk and network, every 2 seconds, 5 updates.
.TP
.B dstat-go \-o stats.csv 1 10
Write CSV while displaying the normal output.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 on usage or runtime error.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR dstat (1),
.BR vmstat (8),
.BR iostat (1)
